{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: \site{r_pred_qd_ful}, \site{r_pred_qd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_qd_na}). The broader sensitivity excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful} fulfilled, \site{r_pred_d_fail} failed, and \site{r_pred_d_na} not assessed (counting direct inputs only: \site{r_pred_dd_ful}, \site{r_pred_dd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_dd_na}). No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by a margin far below the reported precision), so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).
 
