docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:20:A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, the original comparative claim failed; an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family. The second test compares R3 with a verified-history comparator C$^*$ at matched budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks, and repeats the B3--R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:21:Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so neither the ordering nor the equivalence extends to the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, not on attacker gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$; and an exploratory lottery between a welfare attack and a profitable attack, selected on the evaluation records, has estimated normalised gain \site{abs_ph_mix_gain} and gross efficiency \site{abs_ph_mix_eff} against R3, with its population profitability not established. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:22:All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and a population whose honest providers never replace their identities.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:40:\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3: R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:61:\paragraph{Audit budget.} Let $\hat S$ be the expected honest first-best surplus over $T$ rounds and $m = \hat S / n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator's audit budget is $B = \beta \hat S$ and the audit cost $\alpha = \rho m$, so the allowance is $B/\alpha = (\beta/\rho)\, n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ audits; unspent budget carries over.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:63:\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Honest providers never replace their identities, so in this model a new identity is always a deviating one, except in the honest-replacement diagnostic of the second test; honest churn is outside the model (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:67:\paragraph{Benchmark and metrics.} One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly at full effort with truthful claims; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best surplus $S^*$, the focal provider's \emph{gain} is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected truthful reference, divided by $S^*/N$. \emph{Gross efficiency} is $(W + \text{audit cost})/S^*$, with $W$ the realised social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is $W/S^*$. Gain is a difference of two searched values, so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The second test adds two objectives at its headline market: welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker's reference on the training seeds (W1), whose profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95\% lower bound on the evaluation seeds is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain $G_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and by the worst member's mean gain $G_{\min}$, both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results say nothing about coalition stability.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:81:We fix a provider facing honest opponents and an offer with claim $0 < c \le q^{\max}$, and compare truthful effort $c$ with effort $c - \Delta$, $0 \le \Delta \le c$, at the unchanged offer. We condition on the pre-delivery state, rival outcomes, and the audit set, all independent of the focal delivery noise, and assume that focal audit outcomes affect continuation value only through whether any focal job fails, with the same conditional values under both effort levels. Shading saves $g = b\Delta$ per served job and raises the per-audit failure probability from $d_0 = \Phi(-\tau/\sigma)$ to $d = \Phi((\Delta - \tau)/\sigma)$. Let $s$ be the provider's served jobs in the round, $U$ the audited subset with $K = |U|$ and escrows $M_j$, and $C_U$ the difference between the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:89:If, after an immediate replacement at $e = 0$, the distribution of all payoff-relevant future states equals that after no failure up to identity relabelling, and every continuation available after a failure is also available after no failure up to relabelling (both hold for B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation), then $C_U = 0$, and shading pays whenever $sg > (d - d_0)\,\mathbb{E}\sum_{j \in U} M_j$, whatever the ban length.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:96:After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn it would share the newest positions with honest newcomers.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:112:Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
