\caption{Q3 repair block at high audit cost, R3 against R4 and the other rules.}\label{tab:q3-repair}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Secondary comparison.} At the headline market, with each rule's own tuned parameters, the gains are \site{r_sec_b3t} (B3-t), \site{r_sec_r3t} (R3-t), and \site{r_sec_cst} (C$^*$-t) under D2+H3. This comparison is secondary and descriptive.

\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
